The Efficient Frontier of Compute Glossary

Acronyms and technical terms
RiboNucleic Acid (RNA). A single-stranded nucleic acid polymer of ribonucleotides that assists in gene expression.
DeoxyriboNucleic Acid (DNA). A double-stranded nucleic acid polymer of deoxyribonucleotides that stores genetic information.
Application-Specific Integrated Circuit (ASIC). A processor designed for a sole application (mining bitcoin). Maximal performance-per-watt.
Tensor Processing Unit (TPU). Google's processor for neural networks. Built using systolic-array matrix-multiply units and on-chip memory.
Surface Acoustic Wave (SAW). Sound traveling across a piezoelectric crystal regulated by electrodes to filter radio frequencies.
Graphics Processing Unit (GPU). A processor with thousands of cores running in parallel. Built for rendering graphics, now the standard hardware for AI operations.
Generative Pre-trained Transformer (GPT). A decoder-only transformer trained to predict the next token across text. A language model.
Central Processing Unit (CPU). A general-purpose processor optimized to minimize latency on unpredictable instructions.
Electronic Numerical Integrator and Computer (ENIAC). The first programmable, electronic, general-purpose computer (1945). Built from ~18,000 vacuum tubes and programmed by rewiring.
Rapid Single-Flux-Quantum (RSFQ). Superconducting logic encoding bits as picosecond magnetic pulses across Josephson junctions. Energy intensive and quick.
Adiabatic Quantum-Flux-Parametron (AQFP). Superconducting logic running slowly enough that energy is returned rather than dissipated. Near the thermodynamic limit, at the cost of speed.
Thermodynamic Sampling Unit (TSU). A processor built from transistors whose thermal noise fluctuates probabilistically rather than hold a fixed 0 or 1. Samples from a distribution physically instead of computing it.
American Invitational Mathematics Examination (AIME). A 15-question, three-hour US secondary-school Olympiad qualifier with integer-only answers (000–999). Sits between the AMC 10/12 and the USAMO.
