Taxonomy axes
Hardware (columns)
Organic. Computation carried out in living tissue. Molecules and cells as the switching medium.
Charge. Computation carried out by moving or storing electrons, including silicon logic and processing-in-memory. The dominant substrate of the electronic era.
Kinetic. Computation carried out by physical motion. Gears, levers, and cams as the switching medium.
Wave. Computation carried out by propagating waves — electromagnetic, acoustic, magnetic, or plasma. Interference does the arithmetic.
Superconductor. Computation carried out in a zero-resistance material below its critical temperature. Switching happens at Josephson junctions.
Atomic. Computation carried out on individual atoms or ions held in place by fields or light. Every qubit is identical by physics rather than fabrication.
Software (rows)
Fixed. A rule set wired into the substrate and not changeable without rebuilding it. No stored program.
Universal. A substrate that can execute any computable procedure given the right instructions. Turing-complete.
Learned Weights. Behavior encoded in numerical parameters fitted to data rather than in explicit instructions. The rule is discovered, not written.
Probabilistic. A substrate that samples from a distribution as a physical process. Randomness is the mechanism rather than an error term.
Superposition. A substrate holding multiple states simultaneously until measured. Interference between amplitudes performs the computation.
Processing-In-Memory (PIM). An architecture that performs arithmetic inside the memory array instead of shuttling data to a separate processor. Removes the von Neumann bottleneck.
