Compute types
Organic
First RNA (~3.8 billion years ago). The earliest self-replicating ribonucleic acid, able to both carry a sequence and catalyze its own copying. The first molecule read as instruction rather than merely reacted with.
Adleman DNA (1994). Leonard Adleman's test-tube solution to a seven-node Hamiltonian path problem using hybridizing DNA strands. Massively parallel by brute force, with the answer extracted by gel electrophoresis.
Genetic circuits (2000). Synthetic gene networks built to implement logic inside living cells. The repressilator chained three mutually repressing genes into an oscillator.
Engineered cells (2010). A bacterial cell running a chemically synthesized genome, JCVI-syn1.0. The genome was assembled from oligonucleotides and transplanted into an emptied host.
Cortical Labs (2022). Living cortical neurons cultured on a multi-electrode array and trained to play Pong. Feedback delivered as structured versus random electrical stimulation.
FinalSpark (2024). A Swiss platform offering remote compute time on living human brain organoids. Sixteen organoids wired into a cloud-accessible bioprocessor.
Animal nervous systems (~600 million years ago). The first networks of electrically excitable cells, appearing in early cnidarians. Compute detaches from the single cell and begins coordinating a body.
Charge
Hand Calculator (1964). The first fully transistorized electronic calculators. Desktop-sized, with fixed arithmetic in hardware and no stored program.
Bitcoin ASIC (2013). A chip that does nothing but double SHA-256 hashing for proof-of-work mining. See ASIC.
TPU (2016). See TPU.
ENIAC (1946). See ENIAC.
GPU (1999). See GPU.
GPT-4 (2023). See GPT.
TrueNorth (2014). IBM's spiking neuromorphic processor, one million digital neurons and 256 million synapses. Draws roughly 70 milliwatts because neurons stay silent until an event arrives.
Loihi 2 (2021). Intel's second-generation neuromorphic research chip, built on Intel 4. Neuron models are programmable microcode rather than fixed circuits.
Mythic Analog (2022). An analog compute-in-memory accelerator that stores weights as charge on flash transistors. Matrix multiplication happens as Ohm's law in the memory array, skipping the data movement entirely.
Normal Computing (2023). A thermodynamic hardware effort building stochastic processing units for linear algebra and sampling. Physical relaxation to equilibrium replaces the iterative solver.
Extropic X0 (2024). Extropic's first test silicon for probabilistic computing. Validates transistor cells engineered to fluctuate as programmable random bits.
Kinetic
Antikythera (~100 BC). A bronze geared mechanism encoding lunar, solar, and planetary cycles. Turning the crank advanced the model to any date and read out eclipse timing.
Babbage Difference Engine (1822). A mechanical calculator that tabulates polynomials by the method of finite differences. Addition-only gearing, no branching.
Analytical Engine (1837). Babbage's design for a general-purpose mechanical computer, with a mill for arithmetic and a store for numbers. Punched-card programmed and conditionally branching, but never built.
Wave
SAW Filters (1965). See SAW.
Lightmatter Envise (2021). A photonic accelerator performing matrix multiplication in silicon waveguides. Weights encode as interference, so the multiply itself costs almost nothing.
Caulfield Proposal (1989). A design for a general-purpose optical processor, advanced during the optical computing push. Never built, as optical nonlinearity and switching losses proved intractable.
Imec / Mainz (2014). Magnonic logic, computing with spin waves in a magnetic lattice rather than moving electrons. Interference between waves performs the operation and dissipates no Joule heat.
Xanadu Borealis (2022). A programmable photonic processor running Gaussian boson sampling across 216 squeezed light modes. Operates at room temperature, with only the detectors cryogenic.
PsiQuantum Omega (2024). A photonic quantum chipset manufactured on a standard semiconductor line. Targets fault tolerance through volume manufacturing rather than higher per-qubit fidelity.
Superconductor
RSFQ (1985). See RSFQ.
AQFP (2014). See AQFP.
D-Wave One (2011). The first commercially sold quantum machine, a 128-qubit superconducting annealer. Optimizes rather than computes universally, so it runs no general quantum algorithm.
Google Willow (2024). A 105-qubit superconducting processor that crossed the error-correction threshold. Adding physical qubits lowered the logical error rate rather than raising it.
IBM Nighthawk (2025). A 120-qubit superconducting processor on a square lattice with tunable couplers. Built for circuit depth over qubit count.
Atomic
Wineland (1995). The first two-qubit quantum logic gate, demonstrated on a single trapped beryllium ion at NIST. Internal states held the qubit while the ion's motion carried the coupling.
IonQ (2018). The first commercial trapped-ion quantum computer, using ytterbium ions addressed by laser. All-to-all connectivity, with identical qubits by atomic physics rather than fabrication.
QuEra Aquila (2022). A 256-qubit neutral-atom processor holding rubidium atoms in optical tweezers. Qubits are rearranged geometrically between shots, so the lattice itself is programmable.
Not computation
Viruses. A genome in a protein shell, with no metabolism and no processing of its own. Carries instructions to a host that executes them.
USB sticks. Floating-gate flash cells holding charge as stored bits. Retains state without transforming it, which is memory without a rule.
Chemical. Reaction-diffusion systems whose concentration fronts trace solutions to spatial problems. The chemistry runs the same regardless of whether anyone reads an answer out of it.
Reservoir. A fixed nonlinear dynamical medium driven by an input, with only a thin readout layer trained. The reservoir is untrained physics, so the computation sits in the readout.
Slime mold. Physarum polycephalum thickening tubes along efficient paths between food sources. Optimization by growth economics rather than by encoded rule.
Plasma. Ionized gas whose collective oscillations have been proposed as a computational substrate. Dynamics are rich but unaddressable, with no way to write a program in.
Pre-biotic chemistry. Molecular interaction before any sequence was read as instruction. Reaction without abstraction.
Cosmic computation. The claim that physical evolution is itself computation. Fails the abstraction test, since the encoding is imposed by an observer rather than present in the system.
