Miscellaneous
Compute. The capacity to perform computation. A resource, measured in operations, cycles, or energy.
Computation. The rule-governed processing of abstractions. Independent of medium.
Bit. The irreducible unit of information, the resolution of two equally likely alternatives. One binary distinction.
Landauer limit. The theoretical minimum energy required to erase one bit of information, roughly 3 × 10⁻²¹ joules at room temperature. Set by thermodynamics rather than engineering.
Reversible computing. Computation performed without erasing information, so no energy is thermodynamically required to be dissipated. The route beneath the Landauer limit.
Wall-plug energy. Total energy drawn from the grid to run a system, including cooling and power conversion. Distinct from the energy consumed by the switching operation alone.
Efficient frontier. The set of systems that no other system beats on both time and energy per bit. Everything above and to the right is dominated.
Josephson junction. Two superconductors separated by a thin barrier, across which current tunnels without resistance. The switching element of superconducting logic.
Qubit. A two-state quantum system used as the unit of quantum information. Holds a superposition of both states until measured.
Quantum advantage. A demonstration that a quantum machine solves some task faster than any classical machine could. Usually shown on synthetic benchmarks rather than useful workloads.
Gaussian boson sampling. A quantum benchmark measuring photon distributions across an interferometer. Hard to simulate classically but not a general-purpose algorithm.
Error-correction threshold. The point at which adding physical qubits lowers the logical error rate instead of raising it. The precondition for scaling a quantum computer.
Neuromorphic. Hardware modeled on the event-driven behavior of biological neurons. Elements stay idle until a signal arrives.
Organoid. A small cluster of cultured cells self-organizing into tissue-like structure. Used as a living substrate for compute.
Large Language Model (LLM). A model trained to predict the next token across large text corpora. Behavior encoded in learned weights rather than explicit instructions.
Inference. Running a trained model to produce an output. Distinct from training, which fits the weights.
Token. The unit of text a language model reads and emits, typically a word fragment. Roughly four characters in English.
Mixture-of-Experts (MoE). An architecture that routes each token to a subset of the network rather than the whole. Cuts active parameters per token without cutting total capacity.
Model distillation. Training a small model to reproduce a large model's outputs. Captures most of the capability at a fraction of the inference cost.
Sliding window attention. An attention pattern in which each token attends only to a fixed span of neighbors rather than the full sequence. Turns quadratic attention cost into linear.
RWKV. A recurrent architecture in which the influence of a token decays exponentially as the sequence advances. Linear-time inference with no growing key-value cache.
Jevons paradox. The observation that efficiency gains in a resource increase total consumption of it rather than reduce it. Named for coal, applicable to compute.
Watt-hour (Wh). One watt sustained for one hour, equal to 3,600 joules. The standard unit for per-query energy.

